==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|extricatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|extricō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɛks.tɹɪ.keɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-extricate.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|extricat}}

# {{label|en|transitive}} To [[free]], [[disengage]], [[loosen]], or [[untangle]].
#: ''I finally managed to '''extricate''' myself from the tight jacket.''
#: ''The firemen had to use the [[jaws of life]] to '''extricate''' Monica from the car wreck.''
# {{label|en|rare}} To free from [[intricacy|intricacies]] or [[perplexity]]
#* '''1662:''' Thomas Salusbury, ''Galileo's Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems'' (Dialogue Two)
#*: ''Your argumentation ... is invelloped with certain intricacies, that are not easie to be '''extricated'''.''

